First, we find the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of $\mathbf{A}$.

For $\lambda = 0$,
\begin{align*}
\mathbf{A} \begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix},
\end{align*}
which gives $y = 1$ and $x = 0$.

For $\lambda = 3$,
\begin{align*}
(\mathbf{A} - 3\mathbf{I}) \begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} -3 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix},
\end{align*}
which gives $y = -3x$.

The eigenvalues of $\mathbf{A}$ are $\{0, 3\}$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are $\left\{\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \end{pmatrix}, \begin{pmatrix} 0 & -3 \end{pmatrix}\right\}$.

Let $\mathbf{v}_1 = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \end{pmatrix}$ and $\mathbf{v}_2 = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & -3 \end{pmatrix}$ be eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalues $0$ and $3$, respectively.  We will find a vector $\mathbf{v} = \begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \end{pmatrix}$ such that
\begin{align*}
(\mathbf{A}^8 + \mathbf{A}^6 + \mathbf{A}^4 + \mathbf{A}^2 + \mathbf{I}) \mathbf{v} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 11 \end{pmatrix}.
\end{align*}
Multiplying the above equation by $\mathbf{v}_2$, we see that both $\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\mathbf{v}_2$ are in the nullspace of $\mathbf{A}^8 + \mathbf{A}^6 + \mathbf{A}^4 + \mathbf{A}^2 + \mathbf{I}$.  If we let $c$ be any scalar, $c \mathbf{v}_2$ is also in the nullspace of $\mathbf{A}^8 + \mathbf{A}^6 + \mathbf{A}^4 + \mathbf{A}^2 + \mathbf{I}$.  It follows, therefore, that
\begin{align*}
\mathbf{v} = \begin{pmatrix} 11 \\ -11 \end{pmatrix} + c \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ -2 \end{pmatrix}
\end{align*}
is a solution for any $c$.  Choosing $c = 1$ gives the vector $\boxed{\begin{pmatrix} 11 \\ -11 \end{pmatrix}}$. The answer is \begin{pmatrix}11\-11\end{pmatrix}

We start with the matrix $\mathbf{A} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ 3 & 0 \end{pmatrix}.$  Then
\begin{align*}
\mathbf{A}^2 &= \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ 3 & 0 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ 3 & 0 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 3 & 0 \\ 0 & 3 \end{pmatrix} = 3\mathbf{A},
\end{align*}so $\mathbf{A}^2 = 3\mathbf{A}.$  Next, we compute that
\begin{align*}
\mathbf{A}^4 &= 9\mathbf{A}^2 = 9(3\mathbf{A}) = 27\mathbf{A},
\end{align*}so $\mathbf{A}^4 = 27\mathbf{A}.$  Similarly,
\begin{align*}
\mathbf{A}^6 &= 81\mathbf{A}^2 = 81(3\mathbf{A}) = 243\mathbf{A},
\end{align*}so $\mathbf{A}^6 = 243\mathbf{A}.$  Finally, we compute that
\begin{align*}
\mathbf{A}^8 &= 243\mathbf{A}^2 = 243(3\mathbf{A}) = 729\mathbf{A}.
\end{align*}Thus,
\begin{align*}
\mathbf{A}^8 + \mathbf{A}^6 + \mathbf{A}^4 + \mathbf{A}^2 + \mathbf{I} &= 729\mathbf{A} + 243\mathbf{A} + 27\mathbf{A} + 3\mathbf{A} + 1\mathbf{I} \\
&= \boxed{772\mathbf{A} + 1\mathbf{I}},
\end{align*}and the vector
\[\mathbf{v} = \frac{1}{772}\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 11 \end{pmatrix} = \boxed{\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ \frac{11}{772} \end{pmatrix}}.\]The answer is: \begin{pmatrix}0\45,009\end{pmatrix}
